WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.800
(music)

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:10.650
I'm Mike McGehee and I'm a professor in
Material Science and Engineering at

00:00:10.650 --> 00:00:15.680
Stanford University. And I'm the Academic
Director of the Energy Innovation and

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:22.010
Emerging Technologies program. We created
the program because there's a huge need

00:00:22.010 --> 00:00:28.910
to transform the way we generate, store
and use energy, ah, to meet the world's

00:00:28.910 --> 00:00:35.180
energy needs and prevent, ah, climate change.
The curriculum in the program takes a

00:00:35.180 --> 00:00:41.230
holistic view of energy. We have a broad
collection of courses that covers the

00:00:41.230 --> 00:00:50.090
whole energy sector. People can choose
to take some of the more engineering oriented courses, or

00:00:50.090 --> 00:00:53.539
they can take courses that look at the
bigger picture-

00:00:53.539 --> 00:00:59.020
economical aspects and the social aspects. It's
incredibly valuable

00:00:59.020 --> 00:01:03.080
to be learning from people who are right at the cutting edge.

00:01:03.080 --> 00:01:09.460
Many of the best professors in the world who do
research on energy are here at Stanford

00:01:09.460 --> 00:01:16.600
and for each of the topics we were able to
get world-class scholars. It's great to

00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:22.780
be able to take these Stanford courses from anywhere in the
world at any time.

00:01:22.780 --> 00:01:29.560
It's incredibly convenient. People who take
these courses will understand what the

00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:35.080
technology can do today, and they'll get
a pretty good idea of what might be able to do

00:01:35.080 --> 00:01:41.590
in five years, ten years, twenty-five years.
They'll see the competing technologies

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:46.400
and the advantages and disadvantages of those.

00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:52.830
Energy is so important for nearly everything we do.
This program will help people understand

00:01:52.830 --> 00:01:55.350
where the energy industry is headed.

00:01:55.350 --> 00:02:00.000
(music to fade out)

